Credits
-------

This project contains no new hardware support. It carries the POWER9 NPU2
(NVLink2) code that Linux shipped from v4.5 to v5.12 — and that Red Hat
Enterprise Linux 8 still ships — over to kernels that no longer have it,
with the smallest changes needed. The people below wrote that code; any
bugs introduced by moving it are this project's own.

Original NPU / NVLink code (arch/powerpc/platforms/powernv/npu-dma.c and
the NVLink parts of pci-ioda.c), from the upstream history of
npu-dma.c up to v5.12 (65 commits):

  Alistair Popple        NVLink NPU support (5d2aa710e697, v4.5); NVLink2
                         address translation services (1ab66d1fbada, v4.12)
                         and the context API fixes (a1409adac748,
                         28a5933e8d36); ATSD / nest MMU flush ordering;
                         author of npu-dma.c (Copyright IBM Corporation 2015)
  Alexey Kardashevskiy   NPU IOMMU / TCE handling, NVLink pass-through,
                         moving OPAL context calls out of the context code
                         (0e759bd75285), faulting pages for the GPU
                         (58629c0dc349), mmio-atsd bounds checks
  Mark Hairgrove         size-based ATSD invalidates (3689c37d23fc), fewer
                         barriers per ATSD (7ead15a1442b), notifier
                         registration failure fix (720c84046c26) (NVIDIA)
  Oliver O'Halloran      IOMMU group setup for NPU and PCI PEs
  Reza Arbab             ATSD register allocation race (9eab9901b015)
  Frederic Barrat        separate PHB type for NPU2 OpenCAPI links
                         (7f2c39e91f61)
  Michael Ellerman       build fixes, MMIO accessors
  Benjamin Herrenschmidt, Russell Currey, Daniel Axtens, Greg Kurz,
  Jérôme Glisse, Peter Xu, Thomas Gleixner, Heiner Kallweit, Dan Carpenter,
  Bart Van Assche, Krzysztof Kozlowski, Adam Buchbinder, Christoph Hellwig,
  Linus Torvalds — fixes and API updates along the way.

Code this project depends on, already in RHEL 9:

  Aneesh Kumar K.V       256 MiB memory blocks with coherent device memory
                         (4d15721177d5, 4c33bf147249), so GPU memory onlines
                         without losing a 1 GiB block per GPU
  Alistair Popple        secondary TLB invalidation from the architecture
                         TLB flush code (1af5a8109904 and series), which
                         the module's ATSD path hooks into

The firmware side (skiboot NPU2 support) and the GPU driver side
(nv-ibmnpu, nvidia-uvm IBM ATS) are by IBM and NVIDIA respectively and are
used unmodified, apart from the follow_pfn build fix in nvidia-patches/.

Removal history, for reference: 7eb3cf761927 ("powerpc/powernv: remove
unused NPU DMA code", v5.3) and 562d1e207d32 ("powerpc/powernv: remove the
nvlink support", v5.13).
